  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nA stationary front located well to our south in the lower Ohio \nValley will lift north across the area tonight in response to \nlow pressure moving eastward across the Great Lakes region. \nSeveral periods of showers and thunderstorms are expected \nareawide through at least Sunday evening, when the cold front \nsweeps southeastward across the area.\n\nA complex of thunderstorms currently located in central \nIllinois will gradually shift eastward through the day, with \nmost model guidance initially presenting this as light isolated \nto scattered rain showers spreading eastward through the day \nSaturday, well ahead of the warm front aloft. At some point late\nthis evening into tonight, should be convective redevelopment \nas the 850mb theta-e gradient shifts eastward, and the LLJ \nstrengthens behind it. Convection is likely to focus along our \nsouthwest counties tonight. Any convection that develops tonight\nwould pose a flash flooding risk due to PWAT values in the \n2-2.5\" (near a climatological max), which would support \nefficient, heavy rainfall. There is significant uncertainty with\nplacement and coverage of convection tonight, aided by run- to-\nrun variability. Compared to the 12Z guidance, most of the \nlatest 00Z models have backed off on LLJ strength, and QPF \namounts by quite a bit tonight. This trend (and general model\nguidance volatility) precludes the issuance of a flood watch \nfor the time being, though we will continue to monitor latest \nforecast trends to see if one may be needed. If one were needed,\nit is mostly needed for our southwest counties (e.g. Hancock to\nKnox Counties), where the flooding risk is greatest.\n\nWhile heavy rain and flooding risk are the primary weather\nhazards, strong wind gusts and a weak tornado cannot be ruled\nout this evening into tonight, particularly for our southwest\ncounties (E.g. Hancock to Knox Counties).\n\nAdditional convection is expected throughout the day Sunday\nbefore the cold front sweeps through Sunday evening, though the\nevolution is even more uncertain. In general though, expect\nseveral rounds of showers and thunderstorms affecting the entire\nforecast area. High PWAT exceeding 2\" will provide a favorable\nenvironment for heavy rain, leading to localized flooding.\nMUCAPE > 1500 J/kg and deep layer shear around 30 knots would\nsupport severe weather (primary hazards are damaging winds and\nperhaps a weak tornado), though this is a secondary hazard to\nthe heavy rain and flooding threat.\n\nIsolated to scattered rain showers may linger Sunday night into\nMonday morning across Northeast Ohio and Northwest Pennsylvania. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nHigh pressure will bring rain-free conditions to the region\nMonday afternoon through Tuesday night. A weak low pressure\nsystem will move across the Great Lakes region, bringing\nscattered showers and thunderstorm to the region on Wednesday.",
